A tariff reduces imports indirectly — it raises import prices, and consumers respond by buying less. How much less depends on price elasticity of demand for imports.
A quota reduces imports directly — it puts a hard cap on the quantity allowed in, regardless of price or elasticity.

The elasticity connection:
When imports are less elastic (inelastic):

Consumers are not very responsive to price changes.
A tariff raises the price of imports, but because demand is inelastic, the quantity imported barely falls.
The BoP doesn''t improve much because import volume stays high.
A quota, however, forces a reduction in import quantity directly, bypassing the elasticity problem entirely.

So when elasticity is low and the price mechanism is weak, the quota''s quantity-control approach is far more effective at cutting imports and improving the balance of payments.
Simple rule to remember:

The less responsive imports are to price changes, the more a quota outperforms a tariff — because a quota doesn''t rely on price response at all.', 'hard', 'cmlvk3hhh00d904kyc0qmswp3', '2026-02-20T19:04:05.584Z', '2026-03-20T05:00:10.825Z', NULL, '[]');

', '["(ii) & (iv)","(ii) & (iii)","(i), (ii) & (iv)","(i) & (iii)"]', 0, '[]', false, 1, 'Current account deficit 
Growth → higher incomes → greater demand for imported goods and services → import bill rises. Unless exports grow equally fast (which is rare in the short run), the current account moves into deficit. This is a classic and well-tested effect.

Improvement in terms of trade 
Economic growth → higher productivity and technological advancement → country produces better quality, more competitive exports → export prices rise relative to import prices → Terms of Trade (Export Price / Import Price) improves.', 'hard', 'cmlvk3hhh00d904kyc0qmswp3', '2026-02-20T19:04:05.584Z', '2026-03-20T05:05:28.637Z', NULL, '[]');

